hypotheses.statement:
Suppose that the integer depths satisfy $0\le a<p\le v\le N$ and $$\label{graph:range}
 p-a\le v-p+2\sigma N,\qquad
 L\le v-p+2\sigma N,\qquad v-p\ge10\sigma N.$$

With $D_\#$ denoting pushforward under the scalar map $D$, define $$\label{asm:measures}
 \alpha_N=D_\#\bigl(W\mathbf 1_{\Gamma_N}
                          (\mu_1\otimes\mu_2)\bigr),\qquad
 \tau_N=D_\#\bigl(W\mathbf 1_{\Gamma_N}\Theta_N
                          (\mu_1\otimes\mu_2)\bigr).$$

The fixed parameters can be chosen, in the order $\beta,\kappa,\sigma,\omega$, so that some $\gamma>0$ satisfies $$\label{asm:shell-bound}
 \int_{2^{N-1}\le |r|\le2^{N+1}}
                   |\widehat{\tau_N}(r)|^2\,dr
       \lesssim2^{-\gamma N}$$ for all sufficiently large $N$.

Let $\tau_N$ be finite complex Borel measures such that $$\label{asm:criterion-hypotheses}
 \sum_{N\ge K}\|\alpha_N-\tau_N\|_{\mathrm{TV}}<\infty,
 \qquad
 \sum_{N\ge K}
 \left(\int_{2^{N-1}\le |r|\le2^{N+1}}
                   |\widehat{\tau_N}(r)|^2\,dr\right)^{1/2}<\infty.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Let $n\ge1$, let $\rho$ be a probability on a unit dyadic cube in $\mathbb R^n$, and put $R=2^N$. For every fixed $0<\lambda<1$ and all sufficiently large $N$, there are pairwise disjoint sets $E_\alpha$, each a union of depth-$N$ cubes, with the following properties:*

1.  *Their number is at most $N^{C_n/\lambda}$, each has $\rho(E_\alpha)\ge R^{-\lambda}$, and $$\rho\Bigl(\mathbb R^n\setminus\bigcup_\alpha E_\alpha\Bigr)
          \le R^{-2}+N^{C_n/\lambda}R^{-\lambda}
          =R^{-\lambda+o(1)}.$$*


2.  *For $\rho_\alpha=\rho|_{E_\alpha}/\rho(E_\alpha)$ there is a nondecreasing $n$-Lipschitz function $m_\alpha:[0,1]\to[0,n]$, with $m_\alpha(0)=0$, such that every occupied depth-$j$ cube satisfies $$\label{reg:cell-mass}
          R^{-m_\alpha(j/N)-C_n\lambda}
          \le\rho_\alpha(Q)\le
          R^{-m_\alpha(j/N)+C_n\lambda},\qquad 0\le j\le N.$$*


3.  *If $\rho(B(x,r))\le Kr^s$ for $R^{-1}\le r\le1$, with $0\le s\le n$ and $K\ge1$, then $$\label{reg:profile-lower-bound}
          m_\alpha(u)\ge su-C_n\lambda-\log_R K,
          \qquad 0\le u\le1.$$*


*Here and below an occupied cube means a cube of positive mass for the specified class. In particular, for occupied $P\subset Q$ at depths $b\ge a$, $$\label{reg:conditional-mass}
\frac{\rho_\alpha(P)}{\rho_\alpha(Q)}
 =R^{-[m_\alpha(b/N)-m_\alpha(a/N)]\pm C_n\lambda}.$$ Constants depend only on $n$; the threshold for $N$ can depend on $\lambda$.

Let $\rho$ be a source probability and $\nu$ a finite pin measure, both on $\mathbb R^n$, and let $0\le a_0<\cdots<a_J\le N$ be a fixed finite collection of dyadic depths. Suppose pin–source distances on the sets in question are between fixed positive constants. Let $G'$ be a measurable set of pairs. For each $j<J$, suppose there is a measurable set $E_j\supset G'$ and a number $f_j\ge0$ such that every $(x,z)\in G'$, with $z\in Q$ at depth $a_j$, satisfies $$\label{reg:local-fibre}
\rho\{z'\in Q\cap(E_j)_x:
     |\pi_x(z')-\pi_x(z)|\le C2^{-a_{j+1}}\}
 \le f_j\rho(Q).$$ The constant $C$ may be any sufficiently large fixed constant, depending on the geometry and on the fixed angular width to be used below. For every $e>0$ one can delete pair mass at most $(J+1)R^{-e}\nu(\mathbb R^n)$ from $G'$ and obtain $G''$ such that, for each pin $x$, each initial cube $Q_0$ at depth $a_0$, and every spherical ball $B$ of radius $C_12^{-a_J}$, $$\label{reg:multiplied-fibre}
\rho\{z\in Q_0\cap G''_x:\pi_x(z)\in B\}
 \le R^{eJ}\Bigl(\prod_{j=0}^{J-1}f_j\Bigr)\rho(Q_0).$$ An identical conclusion holds without a lower pin–source separation if Equation <a href="#reg:local-fibre" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="reg:local-fibre">[reg:local-fibre]</a> instead tests the perpendicular distance to the full line $x+\mathbb R(z-x)$ at width $C2^{-a_{j+1}}$, provided pin and source sets remain bounded and pairs on the diagonal have been removed.

Let $0\le h\le n-1$, and let $V$ be a Haar-random codimension-$h$ linear subspace of $\mathbb R^n$. For a unit vector $w$, $$\Pr\{\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(w,V)\le\delta\}\asymp\delta^h.$$ If $j\le n-h$ unit vectors $w_1,\ldots,w_j$ have singular values $d_1\ge\cdots\ge d_j\ge0$, then $$\label{ang:haar-multiple}
 \delta^{-jh}\Pr\{\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(w_i,V)\le\delta\text{ for all }i\}
       \le C_{n,j}\prod_{i=1}^j(d_i+\delta)^{-h}.$$ When $h=0$, the probability and the factors with exponent $h$ are one.

Suppose $m:[0,1]\to\mathbb R$ is nondecreasing and Lipschitz, $m(0)=0$, and $m(u)\ge su$. If $q<t<s$, there exist $0<A<B<1$, with $B<2A$, such that $$\begin{aligned}
 m(w)&\ge qw &&(0\le w\le A),\label{prep:contact-first}\\
 m(w)-m(A)&\ge t(w-A)&&(A\le w\le B),\label{prep:contact-middle}\\
 m(w)-m(B)&\ge q(w-B)&&(B\le w\le1).\label{prep:contact-last}
\end{aligned}$$

There is a dimensional constant $C_d$ such that $$A_0=0,\qquad |A_i-A_j|\le d|i-j|/N,\qquad
A_n\ge\chi n/N-C_d\sigma.$$ Every occupied depth-$n$ cube has mass $M(n)R^{\pm C_d\sigma}$. In particular, a ball of radius $C2^{-g}$ contains at most $$\label{test:centre-count}
C'R^{I(g,p)+C_d\sigma}$$ occupied depth-$p$ cube centres, for $0\le g\le p\le N$. Here $C$ is any fixed constant and $C'$ may depend on $C$. For integers $0\le p\le t\le N$ and an occupied depth-$p$ cube $P$, the full conditional probability $\rho_P=\rho|_P/\rho(P)$, rescaled by $z\mapsto2^p(z-c_P)$, satisfies the $S$-ball bound down to $H^{-1}$, where $H=2^{t-p}$, with loss $$\label{test:conditional-ball-loss}
K=R^{A_p-\min_{p\le n\le t}A_n+C_d\sigma}.$$

Let $1\le H\le R$, $K,L\ge1$, and $S=d/2$. Suppose $\alpha$ is a probability in a fixed bounded subset of $\mathbb R^d$ with $$\alpha(B(x,r))\le Kr^S\qquad(H^{-1}\le r\le1),$$ and $\xi$ is a subprobability on $S^{d-1}$ with $$\xi(B(w,r))\le Lr^S\qquad(R^{-1}\le r\le1).$$ Then, with Fourier convention $\widehat\alpha(v)=\int e^{-iz\cdot v}\,d\alpha(z)$, $$\label{test:fourier-estimate}
\int_{-H}^{H}\int_{S^{d-1}}|\widehat\alpha(sw)|^2\,d\xi(w)\,ds
 \le C LK(1+\log H).$$ The constant depends only on $d$ and the fixed bound on the support’s diameter. In particular it does not require ball bounds below $H^{-1}$ or any Fourier decay.

The constant $C_d$ in Definition <a href="#test:good-directions" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="test:good-directions">26</a> can be chosen depending only on $d$ so that the following holds for all fixed parameters and sufficiently large $N$. If $\xi$ is any spherical subprobability satisfying $$\xi(B(w,r))\le R^\sigma r^S
       \qquad(R^{-1}\le r\le1),$$ then every level-zero test, uniformly in the class and the occupied anchor cube, has $$\label{test:exceptional-bound}
\xi(S^{d-1}\setminus G_{0,\mathfrak t}(P))\le R^{-\sigma}.$$

Let $L$ be the largest nominal length in the symbol’s list, or in all lists for a product of symbols, with $L=0$ when those lists are empty, and put $$\label{osc:derivative-scale}
 D_*=R^\sigma\max(1,2^L R^{-50\sigma}).$$ For every fixed $j$, an admissible symbol, or a product of a fixed number of endpoint symbols, obeys $$\label{osc:angular-derivatives}
 \sup_x\|b_x\|_{C^j(S^{d-1})}\le C_{j,B}D_*^j$$ for sufficiently large $N$. The constant may depend on the fixed list-size exponents, parameters, derivative budgets and common angular bounds, but not on the spatial multipliers. On a depth-$a$ cell, the product of all mask factors whose anchors have depth at most $a$ is independent of the spatial integration variable and has uniformly bounded modulus. We call these anchors *reached* at depth $a$.

Let $0\le a\le v\le N$ be integer depths, and let $X,Y$ be separated cells at depth $a$ of one or two classes. Let $m_x(w)$ and $n_y(w)$ be products of the undifferentiated masks at one index $i<J_0$ for two specified indexed descriptor lists. Assume both lists have at most $N^{C_{\mathcal A}}$ occurrences, where one exponent $C_{\mathcal A}$ is fixed independently of $N$ for the whole family under consideration, as in Definition <a href="#osc:symbols" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="osc:symbols">32</a>. Let $L$ be the largest nominal length in either list, with $L=0$ when both lists are empty. For measurable $|f|,|g|\le1$ define a possibly complex measure $\nu$ on the distance line by $$\label{osc:weighted-measure}
 \begin{aligned}
 \int F(s)\,d\nu(s)
 &=\frac1{M_1(a)M_2(a)}
   \int_{X\times Y} F(D(x,y))\,
       (D(x,y)2^a)^{-n_*/2}\\
 &\qquad\qquad\cdot f(x)\overline{g(y)}\,
       m_x(e(x,y))n_y(e(x,y))\,
       d\rho_1(x)d\rho_2(y).
 \end{aligned}$$ If $$\label{osc:shell-regime}
 v-a\ge20\sigma N,\qquad L\le(v-a)/2+\sigma N,$$ then for every fixed $T>0$, $$\label{osc:shell-estimate}
 2^{-a}\int_{2^{v-1}}^{2^{v+1}}|\widehat\nu(r)|^2\,dr
 \le R^{o(1)}\sup\mathsf H_F(a,v)+O(R^{-T}).$$ The supremum has the same cells, class normalizations, lists and smoothing index as in Equation <a href="#osc:weighted-measure" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="osc:weighted-measure">[osc:weighted-measure]</a>. Its symbols are admissible with a derivative budget and common bounds for smooth angular factors depending only on $T,d,C_{\mathcal A}$ and the fixed parameters. All these bounds are independent of $N$. When $f=g=1$, the measure $\nu$ is positive. The assertion also holds on the fixed outer supports with $a=0$.

Suppose $b\in C^\infty(S^{n_*})$ obeys $\|b\|_{C^m}\le C_m A^m$ at the finitely many orders needed below, where $A\ge1$. Let $\Delta\ne0$, $e=\Delta/|\Delta|$, $\Lambda=r|\Delta|$, and $r>0$. A cutoff $\psi$ is assumed to equal one on a fixed neighborhood of $e$ and zero on a fixed neighborhood of $-e$, with uniformly bounded derivatives. For every integer $J\ge1$, $$\label{osc:direct}
 \begin{aligned}
 \int_{S^{n_*}}e^{irw\cdot\Delta}\psi(w)b(w)\,dw
 &=\Lambda^{-n_*/2}
   \left[e^{i\Lambda}\sum_{j=0}^{J-1}
                    \Lambda^{-j}(L_jb)(e)+E_J\right],\\
 |E_J|&\le C_J\Lambda^{-J}A^{2J+d+2},
 \end{aligned}$$ provided $\Lambda\ge1$. Without a cutoff the corresponding formula is $$\label{osc:full-sphere}
 \int_{S^{n_*}}e^{irw\cdot\Delta}b(w)\,dw
 =\Lambda^{-n_*/2}
   \left[\sum_{\epsilon\in\{+1,-1\}}e^{i\epsilon\Lambda}
       \sum_{j=0}^{J-1}\Lambda^{-j}
                 (L_{j,\epsilon}b)(\epsilon e)+E_J'\right],$$ with the same remainder bound. Define global differential operators $$\label{osc:B-operators}
 B_0=L_0^{-1},\qquad
 B_j=-L_0^{-1}\sum_{h=1}^j L_h B_{j-h}.$$ Each $B_j$ is a polynomial in $\Delta_{S^{n_*}}$ of differential order at most $2j$. If, for a fixed $c>0$, $$\label{osc:stationary-regime}
 \Lambda\ge R^{c\sigma},\qquad
 A^2/\Lambda\le R^{-c\sigma},\qquad 1\le A\le R^2,$$ then for every prescribed $T>0$ a fixed sufficiently large $J$ gives $$\label{osc:inverse}
 e^{i\Lambda}b(e)
 =\Lambda^{n_*/2}\sum_{j=0}^{J-1}\Lambda^{-j}
       \int_{S^{n_*}}e^{irw\cdot\Delta}\psi(w)(B_jb)(w)\,dw
       +O(R^{-T}).$$ The extracted remainders in Equations <a href="#osc:direct" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="osc:direct">[osc:direct]</a> and <a href="#osc:full-sphere" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="osc:full-sphere">[osc:full-sphere]</a> are also $O(R^{-T})$ after increasing $J$. All constants are uniform in $e$ in the stipulated geometry.*

*For $r=2^v s$ with $s$ in a fixed compact subinterval of $(0,\infty)$, each coefficient $\Lambda^{-j}(L_{j,\epsilon}b)(\epsilon e)$ has uniformly bounded derivatives of every fixed order in $s$ under Equation <a href="#osc:stationary-regime" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="osc:stationary-regime">[osc:stationary-regime]</a>. Every differential coefficient retains the supports of all the mask factors in $b$.

On separated depth-$a$ cells, for every fixed $j\ge0$ there are bounded measurable functions $f_\ell$ on $X$ and $g_\ell$ on $Y$ and constants $c_\ell$ such that $$\label{osc:spatial-series}
 (D(x,y)2^a)^{-j}
   =\sum_\ell c_\ell f_\ell(x)g_\ell(y),\qquad
 |f_\ell|,|g_\ell|\le1,\qquad
 \sum_\ell|c_\ell|\le C_{d,j}.$$ The convergence is uniform. The same assertion holds in the fixed outer geometry, with a constant depending on that geometry.

Let $\rho_1,\rho_2$ be two regularized class laws, possibly equal, and write $M_j$, $A_{j,n}$, $I_j$ and $k_j$ for their profiles and associated quantities from Section <a href="#test:section" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="test:section">6</a>. Let $X,Y$ be occupied cells of depth $a$. Put $X_1=X$, $X_2=Y$, and let $b^{(j)}_z(w)$ be an admissible endpoint symbol on $X_j$ in the sense of Definition <a href="#osc:symbols" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="osc:symbols">32</a>. Let $L$ be the largest nominal length of a descriptor in either symbol, with $L=0$ if both lists are empty. Suppose that the integer depths satisfy $0\le a<p\le v\le N$ and $$\label{graph:range}
 p-a\le v-p+2\sigma N,\qquad
 L\le v-p+2\sigma N,\qquad v-p\ge10\sigma N.$$ At endpoint $j$, suppose that the symbol imposes the parallel test $[g_j,p]$ at a level $\ell_j\in\{0,\ldots,J_0\}$: for $\rho_j$-almost every $z\in X_j$ and every $w\in S^{d-1}$, $$\label{graph:support-test}
 b^{(j)}_z(w)\ne0
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 w\in G^{(j)}_{\ell_j,[g_j,p]}(z).$$ The superscript $j$ indicates the class used in the test. Assume $$\label{graph:anchor-range}
 a\le g_j<p,\qquad g_j\le a+2\sigma N.$$*


*Form the general independent energy $\mathsf H^{X,Y}(a,v)$ of Definition <a href="#osc:states" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="osc:states">34</a> using these specified symbols. For every fixed $T>0$, $$\label{graph:bound}
 \begin{split}
 \mathsf H^{X,Y}(a,v)
 &\le
 R^{-\sum_{j=1}^2(A_{j,p}-A_{j,a})
       +\min_{j=1,2}k_j(a,p)+O_d(\sigma)+o(1)}\\
 &\qquad\cdot
 \max_{\substack{P\subset X,\ Q\subset Y\\
                   P,Q\text{ occupied depth-}p\text{ cells}}}
       \mathsf H^{P,Q}(p,v)
 +O(R^{-T}).
 \end{split}$$ No separation of $X$ and $Y$ is required. Every child in <a href="#graph:bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="graph:bound">[graph:bound]</a> is formed by restricting the same endpoint symbols to $P$ and $Q$. It retains every original mask factor, including any whose anchor is $p$. Constants are uniform over cells and allowed symbols with fixed list-size exponents and derivative budgets, and with fixed common numerical bounds for their smooth angular factors at the derivative orders used. The $o(1)$ tends to zero as $N\to\infty$ with these bounds, the parameters, and $T$ fixed.

For each fixed cap pair, both the row degrees and the column degrees of the retained graph are at most $$\label{graph:degree-bound}
 D_{\mathrm{gr}}=
 R^{\min_{j=1,2}k_j(a,p)+O_d(\sigma)+o(1)}.$$ This estimate does not require the two cap directions to be separated.

Let $\eta,\zeta$ be finite positive measures on the line and let $\varepsilon>0$. Write $$B_\varepsilon(\eta)
 =\sum_{j\in\mathbb Z}\eta([j\varepsilon,(j+1)\varepsilon))^2.$$ For any fixed $C\ge1$, $$\begin{aligned}
 B_\varepsilon(\eta)
 &\le(\eta\otimes\eta)\{|s-s'|\le\varepsilon\},
 \label{rec:bins-lower}\\
 \sup_{u\in\mathbb R}
 (\eta\otimes\eta)\{|s-s'-u|\le C\varepsilon\}
 &\le C' B_\varepsilon(\eta),
 \label{rec:bins-shift}\\
 \iint(1+|s-s'|/\varepsilon)^{-2}\,d\eta(s)d\zeta(s')
 &\le C' B_\varepsilon(\eta)^{1/2}
                    B_\varepsilon(\zeta)^{1/2}.
 \label{rec:bins-kernel}
\end{aligned}$$ Moreover, $$\label{rec:collision-fourier}
 (\eta\otimes\eta)\{|s-s'|\le C\varepsilon\}
 \le C'\varepsilon
       \int_{|r|\le\varepsilon^{-1}}|\widehat\eta(r)|^2\,dr.$$

Fix $0<\beta<\min(\chi/10,1/10)$. If $\sigma$ is sufficiently small in terms of $d,\chi,\beta$, then, for all sufficiently large $N$, the integer $$\label{asm:entry-definition}
 c=\max\{n\in\{0,\ldots,N\}:
                  \min(A_{1,n},A_{2,n})\le\beta\}$$ is defined and satisfies $$\begin{aligned}
 \label{asm:entry-range}
 &\left\lfloor\frac{\beta N}{2d}\right\rfloor\le c<N/4,\\
 \label{asm:entry-lower}
 &A_{j,n}\ge\beta-d/N
       \quad(j=1,2,\ c\le n\le N),\\
 \label{asm:entry-cost}
 &\min_{j=1,2} k_j(0,c)\le\beta+C_d\sigma.
\end{aligned}$$ If $\kappa$ is sufficiently small and $q_0=\lceil\kappa N\rceil$, the interval $[c,N]$ is nonterminal. Its potentials from Definition <a href="#ledger:potential" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="ledger:potential">46</a> obey $$\label{asm:entry-potential}
 \mathcal V_j([c,N])
 :=\min_{\substack{c\le u<v\le N\\v-u\ge10q_0}}
                         (A_{j,u}+A_{j,v})
 \ge2\beta-2d/N.$$

There is $\eta>0$, depending on the fixed parameters and starting measures, such that $$\label{asm:tv-approximation}
 0\le\tau_N\le\alpha_N,\qquad
 \|\alpha_N-\tau_N\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\lesssim2^{-\eta N}.$$ Moreover $\alpha_N$ decreases to the nonzero finite positive measure $$\label{asm:limit-measure}
 \alpha_\infty=D_\#\bigl(W\mathbf 1_{\Gamma_\infty}
                               (\mu_1\otimes\mu_2)\bigr),\qquad
 \Gamma_\infty=\bigcap_N\Gamma_N.$$ All these measures are carried by the compact set of original distances $\Delta(E)$.

The fixed parameters can be chosen, in the order $\beta,\kappa,\sigma,\omega$, so that some $\gamma>0$ satisfies $$\label{asm:shell-bound}
 \int_{2^{N-1}\le |r|\le2^{N+1}}
                   |\widehat{\tau_N}(r)|^2\,dr
       \lesssim2^{-\gamma N}$$ for all sufficiently large $N$.

Let $(\alpha_N)_{N\ge K}$ be finite positive Borel measures on $\mathbb R$ with $\alpha_{N+1}\le\alpha_N$, and let $\alpha_\infty=\lim_N\alpha_N$. Let $\tau_N$ be finite complex Borel measures such that $$\label{asm:criterion-hypotheses}
 \sum_{N\ge K}\|\alpha_N-\tau_N\|_{\mathrm{TV}}<\infty,
 \qquad
 \sum_{N\ge K}
 \left(\int_{2^{N-1}\le |r|\le2^{N+1}}
                   |\widehat{\tau_N}(r)|^2\,dr\right)^{1/2}<\infty.$$ Then $\alpha_\infty$ is absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure. In particular, the conclusion holds if the two quantities inside these sums are respectively $O(2^{-\eta N})$ and $O(2^{-\gamma N/2})$ for arbitrary $\eta,\gamma>0$.
